# The Bhakti and Sufi Movements: Faith as a Social Force

> Between the eighth and seventeenth centuries two devotional
> currents — the Bhakti movement in Hindu society and Sufism in
> Islam — swept across India. Singing in the languages of the
> common people, challenging ritual and caste, gathering disciples
> in temples, khanqahs, and langar halls, the saints built a
> composite culture that outlived every sultan and emperor.

## 1. Devotion Rises in the South: Alvars and Nayanars

Bhakti — loving devotion to a personal god — first blazed in the
Tamil country. The twelve **Alvars** sang for Vishnu (Nammalvar the
greatest; the woman-saint **Andal** among them); the sixty-three
**Nayanars** sang for Shiva (Appar, Sambandar, Sundarar; the
earliest voice is the woman-saint **Karaikkal Ammaiyar**). Their
hymns — the **Divya Prabandham**, the "Tamil Veda", and the
**Tevaram** — were carried on pilgrimage to Shrirangam,
Chidambaram, and Madurai, and later gathered by Nathamuni and
celebrated in Sekkilar's *Periya Puranam*.

[important title="Why the saints mattered socially"]
The Alvars and Nayanars came from every caste — Brahmans, cultivators,
artisans, hunters, and outcastes — and included women. Their songs
said devotion outranked birth and ritual, a claim that unsettled
orthodox hierarchy and drew temples, and their patrons, into the
movement's orbit.
[/important]

## 2. The Acharyas: Shankara, Ramanuja, and Madhva

Systematisers gave bhakti its philosophies. **Shankara** (c.
788-820, from Kaladi in Kerala) taught **Advaita** — the soul and
Brahman are one, the world of appearances illusion — and founded
four mathas at Sringeri, Puri, Dwarka, and Badrinath.
**Ramanuja** (1017-1137, of Sriperumbudur) countered with
**Vishishtadvaita** (qualified non-dualism) and made devotion,
surrender (*prapatti*), and temple worship the path — his Sri
Vaishnava tradition shaped bhakti in both south and north.
**Madhva** (1238-1317, of Udupi) argued **Dvaita**, eternal
difference between soul and God; **Nimbarka** and
**Vallabhacharya** added dual-non-dual and pure non-dual schools
centred on Krishna.

| Acharya | Approximate dates | Doctrine | Key idea |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Shankara | c. 788-820 | Advaita | Brahman alone is real; jnana (knowledge) |
| Ramanuja | 1017-1137 | Vishishtadvaita | Devotion and surrender to a qualified one God |
| Madhva | 1238-1317 | Dvaita | Permanent difference between God and soul |
| Nimbarka | 13th century | Dvaitadvaita | Souls both different and akin to God |
| Vallabhacharya | 1479-1531 | Shuddhadvaita | Pure non-dualism; grace, pushtimarg |

## 3. Nirguna Saints of the North

From the fifteenth century the northern Sant tradition worshipped a
formless god. **Kabir** (c. 1440-1518), a Varanasi weaver said to be
a disciple of **Ramananda**, mocked mullah and pandit alike in his
sadhukkari verses (collected as the *Bijak*), and his hymns entered
the Sikh *Adi Granth*. The leatherworker **Ravidas** and the
bandit-turned-saint Dadu Dayal (1544-1603) preached the same
nirguna faith.

**Guru Nanak** (born 1469 at Talwandi, now Nankana Sahib; died
1539) fused nirguna bhakti into a new community: congregational
sangat, the free kitchen (**langar**), hymns in Punjabi, and the
discipline "remember the Name, work honestly, share your food."
His nine successors carried the line to the *Guru Granth Sahib*
and the Khalsa.

[definition]
**Nirguna and saguna bhakti** — nirguna devotion worships a
formless, nameless God without images; saguna devotion worships
God embodied — as Rama, Krishna, Shiva, or the Goddess. Kabir and
Nanak stand for the first; Tulsidas, Surdas, and the Varkari
saints for the second.
[/definition]

## 4. Saguna Devotion: Rama, Krishna, and the Varkari

**Tulsidas** (1532-1623) retold the Rama story in Awadhi as the
*Ramcharitmanas*, the most recited text of the Hindi world.
**Surdas** sang the childhood of Krishna in Braj bhasha
(*Sursagar*); **Mirabai** of Merta abandoned her royal household
for her Krishna; in Bengal **Chaitanya** (1486-1533) swept crowds
into kirtan, founding the Gaudiya school; in Assam **Shankaradeva**
(1449-1568) built the Ekasarana faith with its prayer halls
(*satras*).

| Stream | Region | Saints |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Rama bhakti | Awadhi country | Tulsidas |
| Krishna bhakti | Braj, Bengal, Assam | Surdas, Mirabai, Chaitanya, Shankaradeva |
| Varkari (Vitthala) | Maharashtra | Namadeva, Janabai, Eknath, Tukaram |
| Nirguna Sant | North India | Kabir, Ravidas, Dadu Dayal, Nanak |

In Maharashtra the **Varkari** pilgrims walk annually to
**Pandharpur** to the god Vitthala: **Namadeva**'s abhangas entered
the Adi Granth, and **Tukaram** (c. 1598-1650) poured the
movement's last great songs into Marathi. Women saints — Andal,
Karaikkal Ammaiyar, Janabai, Bahinabai, Mirabai, and Kashmir's
**Lal Ded** — made bhakti one of pre-modern India's few public
platforms for women's voices.

