[tip title="Reading the dates"]
Kabir's lifespan is traditional (legend gives him 120 years); the
lesson of the chart is not precision but spread: from Chaitanya's
short blaze to Kabir's long legend, the movement spanned five
centuries and every region of India.
[/tip]

## 5. Sufism: The Path of the Pir

**Sufism** (tasawwuf) was Islam's mystical stream — ascetics who
sought direct experience of God through love. Its master-disciple
chain was the **silsilah**; its hospice the **khanqah**; its
discipline the **zikr** (remembrance of God's names) and, in some
orders, **sama** (audition — devotional music). The disciple
(**murid**) pledged to a spiritual guide (**pir** or shaikh); a
saint's tomb became a **dargah**, visited at his **urs**, the
anniversary of his "wedding" with God.

@diagram tree id="sufi-silsilahs" layout="tree" aria="The major Sufi orders in India and their founders"
  Sufi orders in India -> "Chishti: Muinuddin (Ajmer)"
  Chishti: Muinuddin (Ajmer) -> "Bakhtiar Kaki, Baba Farid"
  Chishti: Muinuddin (Ajmer) -> "Nizamuddin Auliya (Delhi)"
  Sufi orders in India -> "Suhrawardi: Bahauddin Zakariya (Multan)"
  Sufi orders in India -> "Firdausi: Sharfuddin Yahya Maneri"
  Sufi orders in India -> "Qadiri and Naqshbandi (16th century)"
@/diagram

## 6. The Chishtis and the Other Orders

**Khwaja Muinuddin Chishti** (died 1236) settled at **Ajmer** soon
after Ghori's conquests; his dargah became (and remains) India's
most visited shrine. **Qutbuddin Bakhtiar Kaki** (Delhi, d. 1235),
**Baba Farid** (Pakpattan, d. 1265 — his hymns are in the Adi
Granth), and above all **Nizamuddin Auliya** (Delhi, d. 1325) made
the order's rules: live simply, refuse government service and
grants, feed the poor from your gifts, and keep affectionate
distance from the court. His disciple **Amir Khusrau** — "the
parrot of India" — perfected the qawwali and wrote in Hindavi.

The **Suhrawardi** order, led in India by **Bahauddin Zakariya** of
Multan (d. 1262), accepted state service and wealth as tools of
influence; the **Firdausi** order flourished in Bihar under
Sharfuddin Yahya Maneri (d. 1380). In the sixteenth century came
the **Qadiri** and **Naqshbandi** orders: the latter's **Ahmad
Sirhindi** (d. 1624), "Mujaddid of the second millennium", preached
wahdat al-shuhud against the prevailing pantheism and counselled
orthodox rigour to the Mughal court. Kashmir added its own
**Rishi** order under Nund Rishi, loved by Hindu and Muslim alike.

[important title="Sultans at the khanqah door"]
Chishti aloofness made the order popular — and made rulers court
it. Akbar walked to the Ajmer dargah, named his capital Fatehpur
Sikri for the saint Salim Chishti, and Mughal princes and princesses
sought burial near Nizamuddin's shrine. Sufi prestige, not state
endowment, was the order's real currency.
[/important]

## 7. Social Impact and Composite Culture

Both movements preached and sang in the people's tongues — Tamil,
Marathi, Braj, Awadhi, Punjabi, Bengali, Assamese, Kashmiri, and
Hindavi — turning vernaculars into literary languages. They
undermined caste by admitting all devotees; they opened worship to
women; they deflated ritualism in both temple and mosque. Kabir,
Ravidas, Namdev, and Baba Farid singing side by side in the Adi
Granth is composite culture made scripture.

[example]
A Maharashtrian weaver's daughter could walk the Varkari road to
Pandharpur singing Tukaram; a Delhi apprentice could sit in
Nizamuddin's langar; a Punjabi farmer could hear Nanak's Japji.
None needed Sanskrit, Persian, or a priest — and all three met at
the same moral core: the Name above the ritual, love above the
law.
[/example]

[note]
Historians caution against overstating the social revolution: the
saints rarely abolished caste in practice, and many followers kept
caste at home. But as a standing critique of orthodoxy — Hindu and
Muslim — and as a shared devotional vocabulary, the movements
reshaped Indian society more deeply than any dynasty of their age.
[/note]

## 8. Legacy

The streams flowed into later history: the Sikh panth grew from
Nanak's sangat; the Maratha polity rose with a Varkari sensibility;
Mughal liberalism heard Chishti teaching in Akbar's sulh-i-kul;
and modern reformers and nationalists — from Rammohan Roy to Gandhi
with his Ram-Rahim idiom and his beloved Vaishnav jan hymn — quoted
the saints as India's oldest voice of unity. The dargahs of Ajmer
and Nizamuddin, the Pandharpur wari, and the qawwali are living
institutions of that legacy today.

